// Distributed schedule: 4 compute workers, row-band partition over
// the spatial y-loop, sync transfers (pthreads-sync compatible).
//
// Same algorithm as naive.sched.nuc; different mapping. The
// differential test in CI must show this produces bit-identical
// output to the naive schedule for the same input.
//
// Filed as TASK-0341.02.02 AC#3 (cycle 208). Picks up from cycle 206
// (AC#1 naive) and cycle 207 (AC#4 cross-backend differential).
//
// Capability surface
// ------------------
// All transfers are `sync` (no async, no buffer>1, no event
// notification). This matches every tier-1 backend's transfer
// capabilities — including the two sync-only backends pthreads-sync
// and mp-tcp-bufsync (per their capabilities.toml). AC#3 final
// state (cycle 209, TASK-0341.02.02): honest-BLOCKED on all 7
// tier-1 backends — 5 on the 3D wait_slice gap
// (TASK-0341.02.02.01.01), 2 on TASK-0330 (in-Loop w2w Push guard).
// NO 16-jacobi/distributed cells are [[required]] today; see
// nuc-nucleus/e2e-matrix.toml:591-642 for the per-backend [[skip]]
// reasons.
//
// What this cell exercises that no existing required cell does
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// `partition=rows` over a SPATIAL axis (`y`) that lives INSIDE an
// outer `Repeat`-class loop (`t`) reading and writing the SAME data
// symbol (`field`) at different time slices. Each iteration of `t`
// writes field[t] band-by-band on the workers and reads field[t-1]
// (plus its halo strips) from the same workers in the next
// iteration. This is the 2D-spatial-partition + multi-iter-time
// shape — orthogonal to:
//   - 05-stencil/distributed (partition=rows, single time pass).
//   - 11-game-of-life/pipelined (pipeline+buffer across iterations,
//     single compute worker — no spatial partition).
//   - 08-histogram/distributed (partition=rows on accumulator, no
//     cross-iteration edges).
// The plausible-but-untrodden combination is exactly the gap the
// task brief calls out: "halo_inference doesn't reach into Repeat
// body for multi-iteration cross-iteration edges" is the failure
// mode to watch for.
//
// Boundary handling (carried from naive.sched.nuc + prog.algo.nuc)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// y walks 1..H-1 = 1..7 (length 6); 6 / 4 workers = 1 row per
// worker with two workers getting 2 (floor-with-spillover, per
// TASK-0262). Per-worker bands:
//   w0: y in 1..3   (2 rows, takes spillover)
//   w1: y in 3..5   (2 rows, takes spillover)
//   w2: y in 5..6   (1 row)
//   w3: y in 6..7   (1 row)
// (decompose follows numpy.array_split policy; TASK-0262
// floor-with-spillover puts the spill on the FIRST workers.)
// Halo width on `y` is 1 (jacobi5_or_seed reads field[..][y-1][..]
// and field[..][y+1][..]); `extend_xfer_tiles_for_halo` in
// transfer_inject expands each worker's per-tile source range from
// `[source_lo, source_hi)` to `[source_lo - halo, source_hi + halo)`
// (per-axis, signed-saturating at 0 — see transfer_inject.rs's
// docs on the expansion formula). For y the SOURCE range is
// `1..7` (from the algorithm's `for y : 1..H-1` walk); halo=1
// expansion ⇒ `[0, 8) = [0, H)` on this fixture — but the upper
// bound coincides with H only because `source_hi == H-1 == 7` and
// `7 + 1 == H`, not because of a `min(.., H)` clamp. If the
// algorithm later iterates `for y : 2..H-1` the expanded range
// would be `[1, H)`, not `[0, H)`. Per-worker expanded reads on
// the AC#3 fixture (y in 1..7, halo=1):
//   w0 (band 1..3): reads y in 0..4
//   w1 (band 3..5): reads y in 2..6
//   w2 (band 5..6): reads y in 4..7
//   w3 (band 6..7): reads y in 5..8
// The H=W=8 interior of length 6 across 4 workers is tight but
// valid (every worker has >=1 row of own work; halo widening
// stays inside `[0, H)` because halo=1 and source_lo>=1).

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    place load_input       on host;
    place save_output      on host;
    place jacobi5_or_seed  on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };
    place ident            on host;

    // Row-band partition over the spatial y-axis. Same directive
    // shape as 05-stencil/distributed; new territory is the outer
    // `for t` Repeat that wraps this loop in the algorithm.
    loop y : partition=rows;

    // host -> workers: seed broadcast (host loads, each worker
    // reads its band + halo).
    // workers -> host: field gather (final state read out via
    // ident copy loop on host).
    // No async/buffer/event — pthreads-sync compatible.
    transfer seed   : sync;
    transfer field  : sync;
    transfer result : sync;
}
